  "rules": [
    "Model exhibits a strong upward drift bias (approx +5-8 EUR) relative to true outcomes; apply a negative correction of -6 to -8 EUR to the forecast mean.",
    "When sentiment history shows recent spikes (1.0 or -1.0), the model fails to capture immediate volatility and instead predicts smooth linear paths; reduce forecast variance by 40% and flatten the slope during high-sentiment periods.",
    "Model consistently underestimates sharp upward spikes (e.g., Example 2, 3, 5 where true outcome jumped +1.8 to +2.0 EUR) while overestimating gradual declines; apply a dynamic correction that increases volatility weight when sentiment is non-neutral.",
    "Forecast horizon shows significant mean reversion lag; model predicts steady growth even after price drops below recent average (e.g., Example 4, 5); correct by shifting the trajectory back to the historical moving average of the last 10 days if current forecast exceeds that average by >2%.",
    "Sentiment polarity mismatch is critical: when sentiment history ends with mixed signals (positive then negative), model ignores the reversal and continues upward trend; force a correction toward the most recent sentiment value's implied direction."
  ]
}
